-- Opening moves for the first readings of a puzzle. Generated offline; do not edit by hand.
--
-- A key is the sequence of levels the game has shown so far while the player followed the suggestions, opening
-- reading first, as digits joined by dots: 1 Excellent, 2 Very good, 3 Good, 4 Satisfactory, 5 Poor. "5.3.4" means
-- the first graded row was Poor, after the suggested swap it was Good, and after the next one Satisfactory. The
-- value is { slot, slot, swaps }: swap those two slots next, and at most that many swaps are still needed after
-- this one. The entry for "5.3.4" is { 1, 3, 7 }.
--
-- Modules never matter, only positions: module n is whatever sat in slot n of the first graded row. So every player
-- who sees the same levels while following the suggestions is in the same situation, with the same seeds
-- still possible, and the same swap is best for all of them. Perfect never appears in a key because it ends the
-- puzzle, and an Excellent opening has no entry because it already leaves only ten possible answers.
--
-- The table covers exactly the situations with more than 12 Perfect arrangements still possible, where a search at
-- runtime would be too slow; deduce.lua searches everything below that live. Each entry is the swap whose worst
-- outcome needs the fewest further swaps, found by an exhaustive search over all 7,200 seeds (see "The
-- opening table" in the README). Once the player makes a swap other than the suggested one, the key no longer
-- describes the situation and the table is not consulted again for that puzzle.
--
-- The engine takes no facts from this table, only the choice of swap: the surviving seeds are recomputed from the
-- real readings every time (session.lua, deduce), so a wrong entry here could cost swaps, never correctness.

